{-
   A requirement whose head names a constructor and this body's *own* type variable - inventory D13,
   and `compiler/resolve_mir/select.h`'s `selectOpenInstance`.

   `fn (Ord(a)) firstBelow(xs: [a], i: Size, j: Size) -> Bool = xs[i] < xs[j]` is the two-line
   reproduction this came from. `xs[i]` needs `Index(Array(a), ?, ?)`, whose key `?` and element `?`
   are what the functional dependency promises rather than what the arguments say - so they are read
   off the instance that answers, and the deciding position is `Array(a)` with `a` this body's. The
   rule before D13 was that a body may consult the instance table only for a *concrete* head, so the
   two positions stayed open and the call was refused with "no argument decides what v is". The rule
   now is that the outermost constructor is what has to be concrete: `Array(a)` names `Array`, and a
   head matching it with `a` free matches it at every type a specialization binds.

   The bodies below say it in the fixture's own types, so that what is asserted is the rule rather
   than the library's `Index`. The last of them is the half that is not about matching at all: the
   answering instance carries a context of its own, and what discharges it is the *enclosing*
   signature having promised the same thing, which no instance table could.

   A bare variable head - `Peek(a, ?)` - is still refused, and `Reject.OpenHead.yana` is where that
   and the two heads that disagree are asserted.
-}

data Box(a) {value: a}
data Pair(a) {left: a, right: a}

class Tag(a):
    fn tag(x: a) -> I32

instance Tag(I32):
    fn tag(x: I32) -> I32 = x

-- A dependency whose determined position nothing in the member's signature mentions, which is what
-- makes it the question: only the instance can say what `v` is.
class Peek(c -> v):
    fn size(self: c) -> I32

instance Peek(Box(a), a):
    fn size(self: Box(a)) -> I32 = 1

-- The open head, answered from the table. `Peek(Box(a), ?)` at this body's `a`.
fn (Tag(a)) boxSize(b: Box(a)) -> I32 = size(b)

-- The same question where the answering instance has a context, discharged from what this signature
-- promised rather than from an instance: `Tag(a)` of a body's own `a` is in no table.
class Sized(c -> e):
    fn measure(self: c) -> I32

instance (Tag(a)) Sized(Pair(a), a):
    fn measure(self: Pair(a)) -> I32 = tag(self.left) + tag(self.right)

fn (Tag(a)) measured(p: Pair(a)) -> I32 = measure(p)

fn main() -> I32:
    let b = Box {value: 7}
    let p = Pair {left: 3, right: 4}
    return boxSize(b) + measured(p)
